How comprehensive would you say your knowledge of a Linux distribution is, from the kernel up? How familiar are you with low-level system architecture, runtimes and Linux distro packaging? How have you gained this knowledge?
I know RPM well, as I have built RPM packages before. Systemd is also good for me. I am more familiar with the Fedora family than the Debian family, though I’ve worked on both using Foreman and Red Hat Satellite. I have a good base knowledge at the OS/service level, and good knowledge of the kernel and boot level (GRUB) — I’ve had a lot of experience with systems that can’t boot, multi-disk boot setups, mixed GRUB and Windows boot configurations, and corrupted OS recovery. That said, I don’t have hands-on experience with kernel modules or sysctl tuning. I do know /proc and how each process touches files or memory, but I’ve never done strace-level debugging.

I gained the knowledge of RPM when I worked in my second job, as they were building their own software and installing it using RPM and dpkg. Also, with the bank, I had a lot of experience with open source packages that couldn’t be downloaded directly, as they worked on an air-gapped network — so I had to get the code for those packages, build it, package it in RPM, and install it. For Linux in general, it’s a day-to-day job: each time I get an issue or a new task I’ve never done before, I go learn it, then fix it.

